## Why This Is Asked

Assesses your ability to design an extensible data pipeline architecture that remains stable as new formats are added, using registries and factories to achieve loose coupling.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy pattern for pluggable parser implementations
- Registry pattern to map file extensions to appropriate parsers
- Factory pattern to instantiate parsers at runtime
- Interface segregation with minimal, focused contracts

## Code Example

```javascript
interface Parser {
  parse(data: string): Record<string, any>[];
}

interface ParserFactory {
  create(): Parser;
}

class ParserRegistry {
  constructor() {
    this.parsers = new Map();
  }

  register(extension, factory) {
    this.parsers.set(extension, factory);
  }

  getParser(extension) {
    const factory = this.parsers.get(extension);
    return factory ? factory.create() : null;
  }
}

class Importer {
  constructor(registry) {
    this.registry = registry;
  }

  async import(filePath, data) {
    const extension = filePath.split('.').pop();
    const parser = this.registry.getParser(extension);

    if (!parser) {
      throw new Error(`No parser found for .${extension} files`);
    }

    return parser.parse(data);
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new ParserRegistry();
registry.register('csv', () => new CsvParser());
registry.register('json', () => new JsonParser());
registry.register('tsv', () => new TsvParser());

const importer = new Importer(registry);
```

## Implementation Benefits

- **Extensibility**: New formats added without modifying core importer
- **Decoupling**: Registry isolates format-specific logic
- **Type Safety**: Interfaces ensure consistent parser contracts
- **Runtime Flexibility**: Parsers instantiated based on file extension

## Why This Is Asked
This question explores runtime extensibility and undoable actions through a practical pattern common in tooling and scripting domains.

## Key Concepts
- **Command pattern** with undo support
- **Runtime plugin registry** for lazy loading
- **Safe macro/sequence composition** with transactional semantics
- **Isolation and error handling** for plugins

## Code Example
```typescript
// types.ts
export interface Context {
  args: string[]
  env: Record<string, string>
}

export interface Command {
  name: string
  execute(ctx: Context): Promise<void>
  undo(ctx: Context): Promise<void>
}

export type CommandFactory = (name: string) => Command | Promise<Command>

// registry.ts
export class Registry {
  private factories = new Map<string, CommandFactory>()
  private commands = new Map<string, Command>()

  register(name: string, factory: CommandFactory): void {
    this.factories.set(name, factory)
  }

  async get(name: string): Promise<Command> {
    if (!this.commands.has(name)) {
      const factory = this.factories.get(name)
      if (!factory) throw new Error(`Command "${name}" not found`)
      this.commands.set(name, await factory(name))
    }
    return this.commands.get(name)!
  }
}

// macro.ts
export class Macro implements Command {
  name: string
  private commands: Command[]

  constructor(name: string, commands: Command[]) {
    this.name = name
    this.commands = commands
  }

  async execute(ctx: Context): Promise<void> {
    const executed: Command[] = []
    try {
      for (const cmd of this.commands) {
        await cmd.execute(ctx)
        executed.push(cmd)
      }
    } catch (err) {
      for (const cmd of executed.reverse()) {
        await cmd.undo(ctx)
      }
      throw err
    }
  }

  async undo(ctx: Context): Promise<void> {
    for (const cmd of this.commands.reverse()) {
      await cmd.undo(ctx)
    }
  }
}
```

